      "title": "Three Civilizations, Three Environments"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "The excerpts are adapted into modern English from A. P. Maudslay's translation, The True History of the Conquest of New Spain (1908-1916), which is in the public domain. The introduction and summaries were written for EducateOS.",
      "body": "Bernal Diaz del Castillo was a Spanish soldier who marched with Hernan Cortes into the Valley of Mexico in November 1519. He wrote his history of the conquest many years later, as an old man living in Guatemala, and finished it around 1568, nearly fifty years after the events. He wrote partly to argue that ordinary soldiers like him, not only Cortes, deserved credit and reward. The book was published in 1632, after his death.\n\nOn first seeing the cities of the valley: When we saw so many cities and villages built in the water, and other great towns on dry land, and that straight and level causeway going toward Mexico, we were amazed. We said it was like the enchanted places told of in the old tales of knights, because of the great towers, temples and buildings rising from the water, all built of stone. Some of our soldiers even asked whether the things we saw were not a dream.\n\nOn the great market at Tlatelolco: We were astounded at the number of people and the quantity of goods in the market, and at the good order and control that was kept, for we had never seen such a thing before. Each kind of goods was kept by itself and had its fixed place: gold, silver and precious stones, feathers, cloth, cotton, cacao, slaves, food of every kind, pottery, wood, and herbs. Some of our soldiers who had been in many parts of the world, in Constantinople, and all over Italy, and in Rome, said that they had never seen a market so large, so well arranged, and so full of people.\n\nOn how the visit ended: The book goes on to describe the Spaniards' seizure of the ruler Moctezuma, the fighting that followed, and the siege that destroyed much of Tenochtitlan in 1521. Diaz wrote that what he had seen was now overthrown and lost, and nothing was left standing.",
